{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-336222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/assets/3a03159ef7285c7cbb571d8870edce98452b197d178334b4b06277f05a38d876.png)\n\nFind an equation for the charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) **only**, and given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{C}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\n$q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-336222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{50}{3}q=\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{50}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{50}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{3}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{9}{500}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{9}{500}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-50t/3}+\\frac{9}{500}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{125},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ A{e}^{-50(0)/3}+\\frac{9}{500} & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{100}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(t) & =-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-50t/3}+\\frac{9}{500} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}(9-5{e}^{-50t/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=-\\frac{1}{100}{e}^{-50t/3}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{9}{500}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-336222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e557645b5ed75f34cf6a7c8c14b6704f0397b83ee95355b9328d231457db48dd"} {"question" "q-336222" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-336222" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.018-0.01e^{-50t/3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+7q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+7{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+7\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{35}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{35}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-7t}+\\frac{3}{35}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-7t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{3}{35}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{35}$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e557645b5ed75f34cf6a7c8c14b6704f0397b83ee95355b9328d231457db48dd"} {"question" "q-337112" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337112" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{35}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RC circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+9q=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}} coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The given equation is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+9{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+9\\alpha =\\frac{5}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{5}{72}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{72}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}+\\frac{5}{72}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The constant ${q}_{p}=\\frac{5}{72}$ is the *steady-state charge*. As $t\\to \\infty ,$ the charge $q$ approaches the value of the steady-state charge.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{5}{72}$ coulombs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e557645b5ed75f34cf6a7c8c14b6704f0397b83ee95355b9328d231457db48dd"} {"question" "q-337113" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337113" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{72}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.004\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.06$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{100}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.004}=250q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+250q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}q=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{25}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{25}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-25t/3}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{100},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A{e}^{-25(0)/3}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{100} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{19}{500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{19}{500}{e}^{-25t/3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.06$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.06)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{19}{500}{e}^{-25(0.06)/3}≈0.025,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337122" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e557645b5ed75f34cf6a7c8c14b6704f0397b83ee95355b9328d231457db48dd"} {"question" "q-337122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.021"] ["math" "0.025"] ["math" "0.029"] ["math" "0.048"] ["math" "0.067"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337122" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.025"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/assets/6c5ce500d937822081d819e2c02f5fabece926498dd196e65d64dbdaa1dcb0bc.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{Q}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $50\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=50\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=50I=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)=50\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +3\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{4}{125}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{3}{125}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{4}{125}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{125}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=4,Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=4+3=7.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e557645b5ed75f34cf6a7c8c14b6704f0397b83ee95355b9328d231457db48dd"} {"question" "q-337135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"} {"question" "q-337135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072619/state.json" "file_sha256" "8344e871e4d1bf5b2d16de74d7262f2285696c09b1b100e00206876fc4db60ba"}] "usage" {"q-336222" []} "basis" 2662 "retractions" [[17592186229645 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is r